Agentic platforms---multi-agent frameworks, agent SDK runtimes, and visual workflow builders---are increasingly used to automate software engineering pipelines, yet existing benchmarks measure the capability of the underlying language model rather than what the platform adds on top of it, and comparisons across architecturally heterogeneous platforms remain ad hoc. This paper presents `desmet`, an open evaluation harness that drives any agentic platform through the same four-stage pipeline (requirements and design, code generation, test generation, build and deployment) behind a single adapter contract, holding the model, prompts, tools, and scoring constant so that measured differences are attributable to the framework. The harness collects framework-centric metrics---pipeline completeness, tool-call reliability, error recovery, trace quality, and a redundant-tool-call rate---and aggregates them under a DESMET-style rubric. We validate it on five platforms (LangGraph, CrewAI, Microsoft Agent Framework, OpenAI Agents SDK, Google ADK) with one model and one task, plus a cross-model repeat. All five complete the pipeline, but token cost spans roughly 2.4× on identical work, and conversation-history policy---not architectural category or agent count---is the better predictor of that cost. A second model exposes provider- and framework-level fragilities invisible under the first. The harness, scenarios, and reported runs are available as an anonymised artifact.
